CICD_DAYS = {
83:{"title":"CI/CD Foundations","phase":"CI/CD & Observability","status":"published","summary":"CI/CD turns source changes into a repeatable delivery path where validation, build, test and deployment decisions are explicit instead of depending on manual release memory.","architecture":"commit / pull request → CI checks → build artifact → deployment gate → environment → verification","how":["Continuous Integration means changes are integrated frequently and checked automatically so failures are detected close to the change that caused them. The checks may include tests, formatting, static analysis, security scanning and build validation depending on the project.","Continuous Delivery keeps the software in a deployable state and makes the path to an environment repeatable. Continuous Deployment goes further by automatically promoting changes that satisfy the defined gates. Those terms are related but not interchangeable.","The pipeline should build a traceable artifact once and promote that artifact rather than rebuilding different code at every stage. Logs, status and artifact identity are part of the evidence trail, not decorative CI badges."],"commands":"git status\ngit log --oneline -5\n# Typical pipeline stages:\n# validate → test → build → scan → package → deploy → verify","verify":"Start with a deliberately failing test or validation rule and prove the pipeline stops before packaging/deployment. Then fix the change and verify the exact artifact that passed checks is the one promoted.","gotcha":"A green pipeline only proves the checks you encoded. Missing tests, permissive gates or rebuilding artifacts between stages can automate the wrong outcome very efficiently.","security":"Keep deployment credentials out of repository files, scope CI identities to the minimum environment/actions required, protect branches/environments and treat third-party workflow actions as supply-chain dependencies.","lesson":"A useful pipeline makes failure visible, artifact identity traceable and release behavior repeatable.","evidence":"Published Day 83 journey entry; begins the CI/CD & Observability phase."},
84:{"title":"GitHub Actions: Turning Repository Events into Automated Workflows","phase":"CI/CD & Observability","status":"published","summary":"GitHub Actions gives the CI/CD pipeline somewhere to run by mapping repository events to workflows, jobs, runners and ordered steps inside version-controlled YAML.","architecture":"repository event → workflow → job → runner → ordered steps\n                         ↘ needs → dependent job","how":["A workflow is a YAML file stored under .github/workflows/. The on key declares the repository events that can trigger it, including push, pull_request, workflow_dispatch and other supported events. This makes automation part of the repository rather than a collection of undocumented commands living on somebody's machine.","A workflow contains one or more jobs. Jobs are independent execution units and run in parallel by default unless dependencies are declared with needs. Each job selects a runner such as a GitHub-hosted Ubuntu machine or a self-hosted runner you operate.","Steps execute in order inside a job. A step can run shell commands with run or invoke an action with uses. The distinction matters because actions are dependencies in the delivery path: pinning and reviewing third-party actions is part of pipeline security, not merely YAML housekeeping."],"commands":"name: CI\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n  pull_request:\n\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - name: Run tests\n        run: python -m pytest","verify":"Push a branch or open a pull request and confirm the workflow creates a run, provisions the expected runner, executes the steps in order and reports the job result back to the commit or pull request.","gotcha":"Jobs run in parallel unless needs creates a dependency. Assuming file order creates job order can let a deploy job start before the validation job you thought was protecting it.","security":"Use least-privilege permissions for GITHUB_TOKEN, review third-party actions before adding them, pin actions deliberately, protect deployment environments and do not place credentials directly in workflow YAML.","lesson":"The useful mental model is Event → Workflow → Job → Runner → Steps; once that chain is clear, GitHub Actions stops looking like magical YAML and starts looking like an execution graph.","evidence":"Published Day 84 journey entry. The published post defines the GitHub Actions execution chain and a minimal CI workflow."},
85:{"title":"GitHub Actions Secrets","phase":"CI/CD & Observability","status":"published","summary":"GitHub Actions secrets keep sensitive values outside workflow source while allowing jobs to reference repository, environment or organization-scoped secrets at runtime under explicit token permissions.","architecture":"secret stored → secrets.NAME reference → job environment/input → step executes\nrepository / environment / organization scope\nGITHUB_TOKEN → permissions gate → repository API access","how":["Repository secrets are scoped to one repository and are a practical default for credentials used only by that project's workflows. Environment secrets attach sensitive values to an environment such as staging or production and can be combined with environment protection rules so deployment credentials are not available to every job automatically.","Organization secrets can be shared across selected repositories, which reduces duplication but increases the importance of access policy. A secret is referenced through the secrets context, for example ${{ secrets.DEPLOY_TOKEN }}, and can be passed to a step as an environment variable or action input when the step actually needs it.","GITHUB_TOKEN is different from a user-created secret: GitHub creates it for a workflow job and its effective access is controlled by the workflow or job permissions block plus repository policy. Explicit permissions make the trust boundary visible instead of accepting broad defaults by accident."],"commands":"name: deploy\n\non:\n  workflow_dispatch:\n\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    environment: production\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - name: Deploy\n        env:\n          DEPLOY_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.DEPLOY_TOKEN }}\n        run: ./deploy.sh","verify":"Create a non-production test secret, reference it only in the job that needs it and verify the workflow succeeds without printing the secret. Then remove or rename the secret and confirm the dependent step fails rather than silently falling back to a hard-coded credential.","gotcha":"Secrets are masked in logs on a best-effort basis, but masking is not permission control. Transforming, encoding or accidentally writing secret material to artifacts can still disclose it; the safer rule is to avoid printing sensitive values at all.","security":"Choose the narrowest secret scope, use environment protection for deployment credentials, keep GITHUB_TOKEN permissions explicit and minimal, avoid long-lived credentials when short-lived federation is available, and remember that untrusted pull-request code must not gain production secrets.","lesson":"A secret should exist at the narrowest scope that can do the job, and a workflow should receive it only at the step and trust boundary where it is actually required.","evidence":"Published Day 85 journey topic and visual contract: repository, environment and organization secrets; secrets context injection; and scoped GITHUB_TOKEN permissions."}
}
